WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.329 --> 00:00:04.629
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.629 --> 00:00:09.380
It has been a while now since we've talked
about fluid simulations, and now, it is time

00:00:09.380 --> 00:00:14.570
for us to have a look at an amazing technique
that creates simulations with viscoelastic

00:00:14.570 --> 00:00:17.810
fluids, plus rigid and deformable bodies.

00:00:17.810 --> 00:00:22.900
This is a possibility with previous techniques
but takes forever to compute and typically

00:00:22.900 --> 00:00:28.359
involves computational errors that add up
over time and lead to a perceptible loss of

00:00:28.359 --> 00:00:29.359
viscoelasticity.

00:00:29.359 --> 00:00:33.060
I'll try to explain these terms and what's
going on in a moment.

00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:37.630
There will be lots of eye candy to feast your
eyes on throughout the video, but I can assure

00:00:37.630 --> 00:00:39.790
you that this scene takes the cake.

00:00:39.790 --> 00:00:44.980
The simulation phase here took place at one
frame per second, which is still not yet fast

00:00:44.980 --> 00:00:51.320
enough for the much coveted real-time applications,
however, is super competitive compared previous

00:00:51.320 --> 00:00:53.530
off-line techniques that could do this.

00:00:53.530 --> 00:00:55.790
So what does viscosity mean?

00:00:55.790 --> 00:00:58.910
Viscosity is the resistance of a fluid against
deformation.

00:00:58.910 --> 00:01:04.190
Water has a low viscosity and rapidly takes
the form of the cup we pour it into, where

00:01:04.190 --> 00:01:09.750
honey, ketchup and peanut butter have a higher
viscosity and are much more resistant to these

00:01:09.750 --> 00:01:11.490
external forces.

00:01:11.490 --> 00:01:16.550
Elasticity is a little less elusive concept
that describes to what degree a piece of fluid

00:01:16.550 --> 00:01:19.020
behaves like elastic solids.

00:01:19.020 --> 00:01:24.890
However, viscous and elastic are not binary
yes or no concepts, there is a continuum between

00:01:24.890 --> 00:01:30.710
the two, and if we have the proper machinery,
we can create viscoelastic fluid simulations.

00:01:30.710 --> 00:01:36.210
The tau parameter that you see here controls
how viscous or elastic the fluid should be,

00:01:36.210 --> 00:01:39.340
and is referred to in the paper as relaxation
time.

00:01:39.340 --> 00:01:44.659
As tau is increased towards infinity, the
friction dominates the internal elastic forces

00:01:44.659 --> 00:01:48.150
and the polymer won't be able to recover its
structure well.

00:01:48.150 --> 00:01:53.830
The opposite case is where we reduce the tau
parameter to zero, then, the internal elastic

00:01:53.830 --> 00:01:57.470
forces will dominate and our polymer will
be more rigid.

00:01:57.470 --> 00:02:02.460
The alpha parameter stands for compliance,
which describes the fluidity of the model.

00:02:02.460 --> 00:02:08.600
Using a lower alpha leads to more solid behavior,
and higher alphas lead to more fluid behavior.

00:02:08.600 --> 00:02:13.441
The cool thing is that as a combination of
these two parameters, we can produce a lot

00:02:13.441 --> 00:02:20.120
of really cool materials ranging from viscous
to elastoplastic to inviscid fluid simulations.

00:02:20.120 --> 00:02:22.360
Have a look at this honey pouring scene.

00:02:22.360 --> 00:02:23.420
Mmmm!

00:02:23.420 --> 00:02:29.900
This simulation uses more than a 100 thousand
particles and 6 of these frames can be simulated

00:02:29.900 --> 00:02:31.980
in just 1 second.

00:02:31.980 --> 00:02:32.980
Wow.

00:02:32.980 --> 00:02:37.569
If we reduce the number of particles to a
few tens of thousands, real-time human interaction

00:02:37.569 --> 00:02:40.220
with these simulations also becomes a possibility.

00:02:40.220 --> 00:02:45.250
A limitation of this technique is that most
of our decisions involving the physical properties

00:02:45.250 --> 00:02:50.569
of the fluid are collapsed into the tau and
alpha parameters, if we are looking for more

00:02:50.569 --> 00:02:53.069
esoteric fluid models, we should look elsewhere.

00:02:53.069 --> 00:02:58.130
I am hoping that since part of the algorithm
runs on the graphics card, the speed of this

00:02:58.130 --> 00:03:01.230
technique can be further improved in the near
future.

00:03:01.230 --> 00:03:02.380
That would be awesome.

00:03:02.380 --> 00:03:06.400
Admittedly, we've only been scratching the
surface, so make sure to have a look at the

00:03:06.400 --> 00:03:07.940
paper for more details.

00:03:07.940 --> 00:03:12.240
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

